// Somebody else's tower, on its side.
//
// **The one that gives rather than asks**, and it is deliberately the
// first thing most runs meet: a waypoint the player takes and is simply
// better off for is what teaches that these are worth reading. The ones
// that cost something come later in the same list and read differently
// because of it.
//
// **Poles rather than scrap, and the reason is the shelf.**
//
// Scrap was the first draft: it is the thinnest supply in the game, so
// a handful reads as worth stopping for. It also has exactly one
// consumer — a sun-forge, which needs a whole salvage chain — so a
// tower without one is being handed a material it can only *store*.
// A shelf holds one kind, and the opening tower has three shelves.
//
// Measured on the golden recorder: thirty scrap and forty-three produce
// squatting the shelves, the fiber the ropery needed with nowhere to
// land, and the tower unable to afford a dart battery while holding a
// fortune in things it could not use. **A gift the tower cannot spend
// is a jam wearing a reward's clothes**, so the beats give what
// everything wants.
#![enable(implicit_some)]
WaypointDef(
    id: "waypoint.fallen_carrier",
    name: "Fallen Carrier",
    said: "A walking tower, down on its side. Nothing moving. Strip it?",
    take: "Strip it",
    costs: [],
    gives: [CostEntryDef(item: "item.poles", amount: 5)],
    // Quiet work. Picking over a dead machine draws far less than
    // shoving through a thicket.
    provocation: 15,
    // And it costs time rather than ground: the tower stands while the
    // crew work, so this is negative.
    paces: -140,
)
